# Disabilities and conditions

Use positive language and do not label people with disabilities and conditions.

Be familiar with the social model of disability. It sees disability as caused by barriers in the world around a person, not by the person’s body or condition.

Use phrases such as:

- people living with a disability, or disabled person
- people with diabetes
- wheelchair user

Do not use:

- afflicted by
- suffering from
- victim of
- confined to a wheelchair
- handicapped
- diabetic person
- mentally ill
- sick or diseased person

Example: a description of a door opener on a dashboard.

| Before | After |
| --- | --- |
| The front door opener is for wheelchair-bound guests. | The front door opener lets wheelchair users open the door without a handle. |

The device in this example is made up.

## Mental health

Do not describe people as mentally ill.

Use:

- mental health condition
- mental health problems

## Accessibility

Dashboards, notifications and content should be accessible to everyone who needs them. Follow the [Accessibility](../../accessibility/index.html) guidance. Read [Use clear language](../writing-guidelines/clear-language.html#wg-063) on not assuming one way to see, hear or act.
